from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q061-120')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Mathematics',topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(61,'Real Analysis',r'''sₙ=Σₖ₌₀ⁿ1/(k!)² and tₙ=Σₖ₌₀ⁿ binom(n,k)(−1)ᵏ/nᵏ. Which are true?''',[r'limsup tₙ≤limsup sₙ.',r'limsup tₙ≤e.',r'liminf sₙ≥e².',r'liminf tₙ≥e.'],'AB',['Recognize tₙ by the binomial theorem.','Compare the nonnegative series defining sₙ with the exponential series.'],r'''The binomial theorem gives tₙ=(1−1/n)ⁿ→e^{−1}. The increasing sequence sₙ tends to S=Σₖ≥₀1/(k!)², and 2≤S<e because 1/(k!)²≤1/k!, strictly for k≥2. Consequently e^{−1}≤S and e^{−1}≤e, proving A and B. Since S<e<e², C is false; since e^{−1}<e, D is false.''',r'''For a bounded real sequence, its limit superior and limit inferior are the largest and smallest subsequential limiting values; for a convergent sequence both equal its limit. A series with nonnegative terms has increasing partial sums, and comparison with a convergent positive series establishes convergence and bounds on its sum. The exponential series is e=Σₖ≥₀1/k!. Factorials grow at least as fast as one, so squaring their reciprocals reduces the terms, strictly after the first two. The binomial theorem converts finite sums with binomial coefficients into powers of a two-term expression. The standard limit (1+a/n)ⁿ→eᵃ can then evaluate such powers. Establish both sequences' limits before comparing limsup and liminf statements; estimates on individual terms alone may not identify those limiting quantities.''',[('Evaluate lim Σₖ₌₀ⁿbinom(n,k)2ᵏ/nᵏ.','The binomial theorem gives (1+2/n)ⁿ, whose limit is e².'),('Show Σₖ≥₀1/(k!)³ converges and is less than e.','Each term is at most 1/k!, strictly for k≥2. Comparison with the exponential series proves convergence and strict total bound below e.')])
add(62,'Real Analysis',r'Find limₙ→∞ (1/n)[(n+1)(n+2)⋯(n+n)]^{1/n}.',[r'2/e',r'4/e',r'logₑ2−1',r'2logₑ2−1'],'B',['Factor n out of every product factor.','Take the logarithm and identify a Riemann sum for log(1+x).'],r'''Let Lₙ=∏ₖ₌₁ⁿ(1+k/n)^{1/n}. Then log Lₙ=(1/n)Σₖ₌₁ⁿlog(1+k/n)→∫₀¹log(1+x)dx=2log2−1. Exponentiation is continuous, so Lₙ→exp(2log2−1)=4/e. The options giving the logarithmic integral without exponentiation are not the requested limit.''',r'''A geometric mean of positive factors is most conveniently studied by taking logarithms, since the logarithm converts products to sums and nth roots into division by n. For a continuous function h on [a,b], equally spaced Riemann sums converge to its integral, and this applies to logarithms of positive continuous factors. After evaluating the logarithmic limit, exponentiate to recover the original product limit. Factors depending on n must first be normalized: taking n out of n factors produces nⁿ, whose nth root is n. This cancellation explains a prefactor 1/n. The integral of log u is ulog u−u. Positivity ensures logarithms are defined, and continuity of exp justifies passing from convergence of logarithms to convergence of the positive original quantities.''',[('Find lim [∏ₖ₌₁ⁿ(1+2k/n)]^{1/n}.','Its log tends to ∫₀¹log(1+2x)dx=(3log3−2)/2. Thus the limit is 3^{3/2}/e.'),('Find lim (1/n)(n!)^{1/n}.','The result is 1/e by Stirling n!~√(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ. Equivalently the logarithmic Riemann sum for log x has integral −1, with care at its integrable endpoint singularity.')])
CONV=r'''Pointwise convergence controls each fixed argument separately, while uniform convergence controls the largest error over the entire domain. The supremum criterion says fₙ→f uniformly exactly when sup|fₙ−f| tends to zero. Uniform limits of continuous functions are continuous, and uniform limits of uniformly continuous functions are uniformly continuous by an epsilon-over-three argument. Uniform convergence alone does not preserve differentiability: additional control of derivatives is needed, commonly uniform convergence of derivatives plus convergence at one point. Moving bumps can tend to zero at every fixed point while retaining unit supremum, whereas narrowing bumps with shrinking heights may converge uniformly. Boundedness of each individual function does not imply a common bound or a bounded pointwise limit. Test claimed converses with explicit examples and compute a moving maximum when testing uniform convergence.'''
add(63,'Real Analysis','For real-valued functions fₙ on ℝ, which statements are true?',['A uniform limit of uniformly continuous fₙ is uniformly continuous.','A pointwise limit of bounded fₙ is bounded.','Pointwise convergence of bounded continuous fₙ to bounded continuous f is uniform.','A uniform limit of differentiable fₙ is differentiable.'],'A',['Use epsilon-over-three to prove the first assertion.','For false assertions consider truncated identity functions, moving Gaussian bumps, and smooth approximations to |x|.'],r'''A: choose N so ‖f−f_N‖∞<ε/3, and use uniform continuity of f_N with the remaining ε/3; the triangle inequality proves uniform continuity of f. B: fₙ(x)=max(−n,min(x,n)) is bounded but tends to the unbounded identity x. C: fₙ(x)=e^{−(x−n)²} is bounded continuous and tends pointwise to zero, but sup fₙ=1. D: fₙ(x)=√(x²+1/n) is differentiable and |fₙ(x)−|x||≤1/√n, giving uniform convergence to |x|, not differentiable at zero. Only A is true.''',CONV,[('Does x/n converge uniformly on ℝ?','For each fixed x, x/n→0. But sup over ℝ of |x/n| is infinite for every n, so convergence is not uniform.'),('Show sin(x)/n converges uniformly on ℝ.','The error is at most 1/n for every x, and equals 1/n at suitable points. Its supremum tends to zero, proving uniform convergence.')])
add(64,'Real Analysis',r'''f is bounded and C² on (0,∞), with f″≥0. Which are true?''',[r'f′(x)≤0 for all x>0.',r'limₓ→∞f′(x)=0.',r'limₓ→∞xf′(x) need not exist.',r'limₓ→∞xf′(x)=0.'],'ABD',['Convexity makes f′ nondecreasing; a positive derivative would force unbounded growth.','Set g=−f′, which is nonnegative decreasing, and estimate xg(x) by a tail integral.'],r'''Since f′ is nondecreasing, f′(a)>0 would imply f(x)≥f(a)+(x−a)f′(a)→∞, impossible. Thus f′≤0, proving A. Its increasing limit L≤0 exists; if L<0, the function decreases at least linearly and is unbounded below, so L=0, proving B. The bounded decreasing f has a finite limit ℓ. With g=−f′ nonnegative decreasing, (x/2)g(x)≤∫_{x/2}^xg(t)dt=f(x/2)−f(x)→ℓ−ℓ=0. Hence xf′(x)→0, proving D and refuting C.''',r'''A twice differentiable function is convex on an interval if its second derivative is nonnegative, which implies its first derivative is nondecreasing. Supporting-line inequalities show that a positive slope on an unbounded right interval forces unbounded growth. A bounded convex function there must therefore be decreasing, with a finite limit and derivative tending to zero. The stronger estimate xf′(x)→0 uses the monotonicity of the nonnegative function g=−f′ and finite tail integrals. On [x/2,x], g(t)≥g(x), so (x/2)g(x) is bounded by that shrinking tail integral. Mere convergence of f′ to zero does not generally imply xf′ tends to zero without extra structure; convexity and boundedness supply it here. State both upper and lower boundedness when ruling out a negative limiting slope.''',[('Check the conclusions for f(x)=e^{−x}.','f is bounded, f′=−e^{−x}≤0, f″=e^{−x}>0, and both f′ and xf′ tend to zero.'),('Can f(x)=−log x be a counterexample to xf′→0 under these hypotheses?','It has f″=1/x²>0 and xf′=−1, but it is unbounded, so it violates the boundedness hypothesis and is not a counterexample.')])
add(65,'Real Analysis',r'''fₙ(x)=e^{−n²x²}/n on ℝ. Which are true?''',[r'fₙ→0 uniformly on ℝ and fₙ′→0 uniformly on (−M,M) for some M>0.',r'fₙ→0 uniformly on ℝ and fₙ′→0 pointwise on ℝ.',r'fₙ→0 uniformly on ℝ and fₙ′ does not tend pointwise to 0 on ℝ.',r'fₙ→0 pointwise but not uniformly on ℝ.'],'B',['The function height is at most 1/n.','The derivative is −2nx e^{−n²x²}; its maximum occurs at a moving x of order 1/n.'],r'''Since 0≤fₙ≤1/n with equality at zero, fₙ→0 uniformly. Also fₙ′(x)=−2nx e^{−n²x²}; at x=0 it is zero, and for fixed x≠0 the exponential decay dominates n, so it tends to zero. But at x=1/(√2n), |fₙ′|=√(2/e). This point belongs to any fixed interval (−M,M) for all large n, preventing uniform derivative convergence there. Thus only B holds.''',CONV,[('For gₙ(x)=e^{−nx²}/n, test uniform convergence of gₙ and its derivative.','sup|gₙ|=1/n. Its derivative is −2xe^{−nx²}, with maximum √(2/(en))→0. Both converge uniformly to zero on ℝ.'),('For hₙ(x)=sin(nx)/n, does hₙ′ converge pointwise everywhere?','hₙ converges uniformly since |hₙ|≤1/n. But hₙ′=cos(nx) equals 1 at zero and oscillates at many points, so it does not converge pointwise to zero everywhere.')])
add(66,'Real Analysis',r'''f,g are Riemann integrable on [a,b], with g([a,b])⊆[a,b]. Which are necessarily true?''',['f∘g is Riemann integrable.','If g(x)≠0 everywhere, f/g is Riemann integrable.',r'√(f²+g²) is Riemann integrable.','f∘g is Riemann integrable if both f and g are continuous.'],'CD',['Riemann-integrable functions form an algebra, and continuous outer functions preserve integrability.','Nonvanishing without continuity need not provide a positive lower bound for |g|.'],r'''C is true because f²+g² is Riemann integrable and nonnegative; its bounded range permits composition with continuous square root. D follows because a composition of continuous functions is continuous and thus integrable on a compact interval. A is false on [0,1]: let f(0)=1,f(x)=0 for x>0 and let g be Thomae’s function (0 at irrationals, 1/q at a reduced rational p/q). Both are Riemann integrable but f∘g is 1 on irrationals and 0 on rationals, nowhere continuous and not integrable. B is false: f≡1, g(0)=1,g(x)=x for x>0. Then g never vanishes and is integrable, but f/g is unbounded near zero.''',r'''A bounded function on a compact interval is Riemann integrable exactly when its set of discontinuities has Lebesgue measure zero. Sums and products of Riemann-integrable functions remain integrable, and a continuous function composed on the outside with a Riemann-integrable function remains integrable over its bounded range. Two arbitrary Riemann-integrable functions need not have an integrable composition, because the inner function can map a large set onto a discontinuity of the outer function. A quotient is integrable if its denominator is bounded away from zero, but pointwise nonvanishing alone does not ensure this bound for a discontinuous denominator. Continuous nonzero functions on a compact interval do have such a bound by the extreme value theorem. Remember that Riemann integrability requires boundedness.''',[('If f is Riemann integrable, is |f| integrable?','Yes: the absolute-value map is continuous on the bounded range of f, so continuous composition preserves Riemann integrability.'),('Let g continuous and strictly positive on [0,1], and f integrable. Is f/g integrable?','The extreme value theorem gives min g>0. Then 1/g is continuous and bounded, and the product f·(1/g) is Riemann integrable.')])
add(67,'Real Analysis',r'''Let μ be Lebesgue measure, f≥0 measurable on ℝ, and 0=a₀<a₁<a₂<⋯ be unbounded with aₙ₊₁≤caₙ for n≥1. Let Aₖ={x:aₖ≤f(x)<aₖ₊₁}. Which are true?''',[r'If f is integrable, Σaₖμ(Aₖ)<∞.',r'If Σaₖμ(Aₖ)<∞, f is integrable.',r'If Σaₖμ(Aₖ)<∞ and f≥a₁ everywhere, f is integrable.',r'If Σaₖμ(Aₖ)<∞ and f is bounded, f is integrable.'],'AC',['The lower simple approximation Σaₖ1_Aₖ is bounded by f.','For k≥1 the upper approximation is at most c times the lower; the exceptional low-value region A₀ is the issue.'],r'''Let S=Σaₖ1_{Aₖ}. Then S≤f, so ∫S=Σaₖμ(Aₖ)≤∫f; A holds. On ⋃ₖ≥₁Aₖ, f≤cS. If f≥a₁ everywhere, A₀ is empty and ∫f≤c∫S<∞, proving C (on all ℝ those premises actually cannot occur for a strictly positive lower bound, but the implication is valid). For B and D take f≡a₁/2. It is bounded and belongs everywhere to A₀; the weighted simple function is identically zero and the series is zero, while ∫ℝf=∞. Thus neither converse nor boundedness alone controls A₀. The term a₀μ(A₀) is the integral of the zero simple-function term and is zero even on an infinite-measure set.''',r'''For a nonnegative measurable function, the Lebesgue integral is defined through increasing nonnegative simple-function approximations. Disjoint level sets allow the integral of a step approximation to be written as a sum of level heights times their measures. Monotonicity gives a lower integral bound whenever the step approximation is below the function. A controlled ratio between consecutive positive levels also gives an upper bound on levels above the first positive threshold. The zero-height lowest bin requires separate attention: it contributes nothing to the lower approximation while a small positive function on an infinite-measure region can still have infinite integral. Boundedness implies integrability only on a finite-measure support, not on the whole real line. Treat zero-height terms via their simple-function integrals rather than an undefined numerical product.''',[('If 0≤f≤1 and f vanishes outside [0,3], prove integrability.','The bound ∫f≤μ([0,3])=3 gives finite integral. Finite support measure supplies the missing low-value control.'),('For f≡1/2 on ℝ and dyadic levels 0,1,2,4,…, compute the lower-step integral.','Every value is in the zero-height first bin, so the lower step is zero and its integral is zero, but the integral of f over ℝ is infinite.')])
add(68,'Multivariable Calculus',r'''A nonzero C² function f:ℝ²→ℝ satisfies f(tx₁,tx₂)=t³f(x₁,x₂) for t>0. Which are necessarily true?''',[r'3f_x₁(1,1)+3f_x₂(1,1)=f(1,1).',r'f_x₁(1,−1)−f_x₂(1,−1)=3f(1,−1).',r'x₁²f_x₁x₁+x₂²f_x₂x₂+2x₁x₂f_x₁x₂=6f.',r'x₁²f_x₁x₁+x₂²f_x₂x₂+2x₁x₂²f_x₁x₂=9f.'],'BC',['Differentiate the homogeneity identity once and twice with respect to t at t=1.','The first derivative gives degree 3 and the second gives 3·2=6.'],r'''Differentiate f(tx)=t³f(x) at t=1: x₁f_x₁+x₂f_x₂=3f. At (1,−1) this is exactly B. Differentiating twice gives x₁²f_x₁x₁+2x₁x₂f_x₁x₂+x₂²f_x₂x₂=6f, proving C. A has the degree factor reversed: for f=x₁³ its left side at (1,1) is 9 and right side 1. The same f at (1,0) gives D left side 6 and right side 9, so D is false; its printed cross coefficient also differs from the homogeneity formula.''',r'''A function homogeneous of degree m obeys f(tx)=tᵐf(x) for positive t. Euler's homogeneous-function theorem follows by differentiating in t at one: x·∇f=mf. For a twice differentiable homogeneous function, a second derivative along the radial line gives xᵀH_f(x)x=m(m−1)f(x). The mixed Hessian entries agree under continuous second derivatives, so their contribution in two dimensions is 2x₁x₂f_x₁x₂. These identities hold without requiring f to be a polynomial. Differentiation with respect to a scaling parameter is often simpler than differentiating the first Euler identity coordinate by coordinate. For claims with wrong coefficients, a single homogeneous monomial gives an explicit counterexample. Carefully distinguish multiplying the derivative by m from multiplying the function value by m.''',[('For f(x,y)=x²y, verify Euler’s identity.','f_x=2xy and f_y=x², so xf_x+yf_y=2x²y+x²y=3f.'),('For f(x,y)=x²+y², compute xᵀH_fx.','The Hessian is 2I. Thus the radial second derivative is 2(x²+y²)=2f, agreeing with m(m−1)=2 for degree m=2.')])
add(69,'Multivariable Calculus',r'''F:M₂(ℝ)→S, where S is the symmetric matrices, is F(X)=XXᵀ. Let DF_A be its derivative at A. Which are true?''',[r'If AAᵀ=I, DF_A is surjective.',r'If AAᵀ=I, DF_A need not be surjective.',r'If A is invertible, DF_A is surjective.',r'If A is not invertible, DF_A is surjective.'],'AC',['Expand F(A+H) and retain the terms linear in H.','For invertible A and symmetric B choose H=(1/2)B(Aᵀ)⁻¹.'],r'''F(A+H)−F(A)=HAᵀ+AHᵀ+HHᵀ, so DF_A(H)=HAᵀ+AHᵀ. If A is invertible, for symmetric B set H=(1/2)B(Aᵀ)⁻¹; then HAᵀ=B/2 and AHᵀ=B/2, yielding B. Thus C holds, and AAᵀ=I implies invertibility, proving A and refuting B. If A is singular, take nonzero v with Aᵀv=0. Every derivative output satisfies vᵀDF_A(H)v=0, whereas vᵀ(vvᵀ)v=‖v‖⁴>0. Hence it is not onto, refuting D.''',r'''The derivative of a map between finite-dimensional vector spaces is its linear first-order approximation, with an error that is little-o of the increment norm. For polynomial matrix maps, expansion identifies the linear terms directly, and products of two increments form a quadratic remainder. The map X↦XXᵀ always has symmetric outputs, and its derivative sends H to HAᵀ+AHᵀ. Surjectivity can be proved constructively by solving this equation for every symmetric target. If A is invertible, multiplication by its inverse gives a solution. If A is singular, a vector in the left nullspace imposes a linear restriction on every output, providing an obstruction. Surjectivity depends on the derivative at the specified base point, and should not be confused with surjectivity of the original nonlinear map.''',[('Find DF_I(H) for F(X)=XXᵀ.','At A=I the derivative is H+Hᵀ. Every symmetric B is attained by H=B/2, so it is onto the symmetric matrices.'),('Find DF_0 and decide whether it is surjective.','The linear terms vanish at zero, so DF_0(H)=0 for every H. Its image is {0}, not the full symmetric-matrix space.')])
add(70,'Functional Analysis',r'''On C[0,1] with supremum norm, T(f)(x)=∫₀ˣf(t)dt. Which are true?''',[r'There is α∈(0,1) with ‖Tf−Tg‖≤α‖f−g‖ for all f,g.',r'There is α∈(0,1) with ‖T²f−T²g‖≤α‖f−g‖ for all f,g.',r'{f:Tf=f} is a singleton.',r'‖Tⁿ‖→∞.'],'BC',['The constant function 1 determines ‖T‖.','Repeated integration gives a kernel with factorial decay; fixed points solve f′=f with f(0)=0.'],r'''‖Tf‖≤‖f‖, and T1=x has norm 1, so ‖T‖=1 and A is false. Repeated integration yields Tⁿf(x)=1/(n−1)!∫₀ˣ(x−t)^{n−1}f(t)dt. Thus ‖Tⁿ‖≤1/n!, with equality for f≡1 at x=1. In particular ‖T²‖=1/2<1, proving B, and ‖Tⁿ‖→0, refuting D. If Tf=f, the fundamental theorem gives f′=f and f(0)=0, forcing f≡0. Hence the fixed-point set is the singleton {0}, proving C.''',r'''A bounded linear operator on a normed space has operator norm ‖T‖=sup_{‖f‖≤1}‖Tf‖. A strict contraction has Lipschitz constant below one; for a linear operator this is equivalent to norm below one. An operator may fail to be a contraction while one of its powers is a contraction. The Volterra integration operator gains factorial denominators under repeated integration, because integrating over ordered simplices gives the kernel (x−t)^{n−1}/(n−1)!. Upper bounds on its norm are sharp when attained by a constant function of unit norm. A fixed-point equation involving an integral can often be differentiated, but the integral also supplies an initial value at its lower endpoint. That initial value is essential for excluding nonzero solutions of the resulting differential equation.''',[('For T on C[0,2], compute ‖T²‖.','The repeated kernel gives ‖T²f‖≤(2²/2)‖f‖=2‖f‖. The constant 1 attains this at x=2, so the norm is 2.'),('Solve f(x)=1+∫₀ˣf(t)dt on [0,1].','Differentiation gives f′=f and the equation at zero gives f(0)=1. Therefore f=eˣ, which directly satisfies the integral equation.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
